cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Exsecutor or its compiler, please cite it."
title: "Exsecutor: a systems language in which locale and target are capabilities, and its freestanding compiler"
abstract: >-
  A systems language whose thesis is that no operation may implicitly read the host
  locale, encoding, byte order, pointer width, clock, or environment: if a computation
  depends on a human language or on a machine, that dependency appears in its signature
  or the program does not compile. Capability rows travel with function types and with
  capability-bearing types, so a caller and an audit see the same authority; public names
  must decompose against a versioned Latin morpheme table. The compiler, exsc, is written
  in freestanding x86-64 assembly with no libc and a closed nine-syscall allowlist, which
  makes the purity contract a checkable property of the binary rather than a discipline:
  the emitted program is audited against the capability closure it declares.
type: software
version: "0.4"
date-released: 2026-09-10
